$\underbrace{\sin(~\overbrace{8-9x}^{\text{inner}}~)}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $\sin(8-9x)=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{8-9x} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=\sin(x)&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{-9}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{\cos(x)}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{\cos(\goldD{8-9x})} \cdot \maroonD{-9}
\\\\
&=-9\cos(8-9x)
\end{align}$
